  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n- Deepening fall system to move through the Great Lakes\n\nTwo distinct upper shortwave troughs can be seen this afternoon in\nsatellite imagery, one over the Central Rockies and the other \nmoving from the Canadian Rockies into the Canadian Prairies. Both\nof these shortwaves will move towards the Great Lakes and phase\nwith one another as they approach from the west. Rain will spread\nin towards daybreak on Thursday. The most widespread rain will\nmove Thursday afternoon and evening as the right entrance region\nof and upper jet moves overhead. A low level jet will impact\nsouthern portions of the state, towards I-94 especially. 850mb\nmoisture transport is very strong Thursday evening into Thursday\nnight. All areas will see rain with one half to one inch of rain \nlikely. Cannot rule out some thunder as the cold front swings\nthrough, but the better instability will remain off to our south. \n\nLake effect rain showers will combine with diurnal rain showers on\nFriday for another wet day. Delta T's will rise into the upper\nteens C which will result in MUCAPE values over the lake on the\norder of 600 j/kg. Feel waterspouts are a possibility Friday\nmorning before the stronger winds move in during the afternoon.  \n\nWe are looking at gale force winds on Lake Michigan, but on the\nland we may not meet advisory criteria with wind gusts expected to\nbe in the 30-40 mph range as of now.\n\n- Mainly dry this weekend and into early next week\n\nRidging will take over for the weekend and early next week. We are\nlooking at a dry forecast from Saturday all the way into\nWednesday. BUFKIT profiles show a very dry atmosphere so there is\nfairly high confidence we remain dry Saturday-Wednesday.\n\n- Cool temperatures this weekend then moderating some next week\n\nWe will cool into the 50s for highs this weekend as high pressure\nmoves through the Great Lakes. Lows in the 30s are anticipated for\nSaturday morning and Sunday morning. We are likely looking at\nfrost and freeze headlines at least on Saturday.",
